**user**:
> What is good formatting in LaTeX for a range that spans 0? I currently have, e.g., `-2.8--+11.9\%`, but the first minus is very small compared to the + which is huge.
**assistant**:
> In LaTeX, formatting for numerical ranges, especially those that span zero, should ensure consistent visual appearance and clarity. Normally, a single hyphen (`-`) is used for negative signs, which indeed might appear smaller compared to the plus sign (`+`). To address this, you can use the `\pm` command for plus-minus ranges where appropriate, but in your case, you might want to stick with a uniform approach.
> 
> For a range like "-2.8 to +11.9%", consider using `\text{}` to control the format and make it visually balanced. Here is an example of how you can format this in a LaTeX document:
> 
> ```latex
> \documentclass{article}
> \usepackage{amsmath}
> 
> \begin{document}
> 
> % Use for numerical range spanning zero
> \[
> \text{-2.8\% to } +\text{11.9\%}
> \]
> 
> % Alternatively, you can try this approach
> \[
> {-2.8\% \text{ to } +11.9\%}
> \]
> 
> \end{document}
> ```
> 
> The `\text{}` command helps ensure that the text inside is displayed in the main text font, keeping the size consistent. For the signs, ensure that the `+` is set at the same math level as the `-` by positioning it appropriately in the expression. Depending on your document's font and desired aesthetic, either approach can improve visual balance. Adjust the spacing slightly via `\,` or `\;` commands if spacing needs fine-tuning.
**user**:
> Math mode isn't the problem here. This is all in the regular text.
**assistant**:
> Got it! If it’s all in regular text, you can use custom formatting to ensure both signs appear balanced. Here's a way to format your range while keeping it in text mode:
> 
> ```latex
> \documentclass{article}
> 
> \begin{document}
> 
> % Balanced formatting in text mode
> \text{-2.8\% to +11.9\%}
> 
> \end{document}
> ```
> 
> If you want to enhance the visual appeal, you can use some typographic tricks, such as `\text{-}` and `\text{+}` to explicitly set the signs. Here's a refined example with explicit spacing:
> 
> ```latex
> \documentclass{article}
> 
> \begin{document}
> 
> % Using explicit spacing
> \text{-2.8\% \, to \, +11.9\%}
> 
> \end{document}
> ```
> 
> This uses `\,` to add a small space between the elements, improving readability without altering the mathematical context. If the difference in the size is still an issue, consider using the `\mathbf{}` command to make everything bold, which can help with uniformity:
> 
> ```latex
> \documentclass{article}
> 
> \begin{document}
> 
> \textbf{-2.8\% to +11.9\%}
> 
> \end{document}
> ```
> 
> This approach maintains clarity in text mode while also ensuring that the formatting complements your overall document style.
